+        // ---- recovering from a failed attempt ---------------------------
+        // A wrong password must leave a working prompt. It did not: asking
+        // for a new session from inside onAuthFailure is a no-op, and the
+        // greeter then sat on "Authenticating…" until a reboot (seen in the
+        // transporter VM 2026-11-05, quickshell 0.3.2 + greetd 0.10.3).
+        //
+        // Why, from quickshell's services/greetd/connection.cpp: on an
+        // auth_error it emits authFailure FIRST, while its state is still
+        // Authenticating — so createSession() there returns without sending
+        // anything — and only THEN sends cancel_session and goes Inactive.
+        // greetd answers that cancel with an error of its own ("unable to
+        // send message: Connection refused": the PAM worker is already
+        // gone), and quickshell goes Inactive once more on receiving it.
+        //
+        // So the new session may only be requested AFTER that reply has been
+        // handled; asked for any sooner, the reply would knock the new
+        // session's state back to Inactive and the eventual success would be
+        // dropped as unexpected. Hence: wait for the error, then one more
+        // trip round the event loop (Qt.callLater). The timer covers a cancel
+        // that succeeds, which produces no signal at all.
+        //
+        // `busy` stays true meanwhile, so nothing can be typed into a prompt
+        // that has no session behind it; the next authMessage re-opens it.
+        property bool recovering: false
+
+        function recoverSession() {
+            if (!win.recovering)
+                return;
+            win.recovering = false;
+            recoverTimeout.stop();
             Greetd.createSession(GreeterConfig.user);
         }
 
+        Timer {
+            id: recoverTimeout
+            interval: 2000
+            onTriggered: win.recoverSession()
+        }
